1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,309
Cath lab skills, one real case at a time.

2
00:00:03,759 --> 00:00:08,603
Today we'll work through a pharmaco-invasive PCI to the LAD, step by step.

3
00:00:08,603 --> 00:00:16,261
How to think about the case, how to read the angiogram, why each piece of hardware was chosen, and what can go wrong.

4
00:00:16,711 --> 00:00:20,620
The case is de-identified, and this is teaching material only.

5
00:00:20,620 --> 00:00:25,223
Learn and operate under supervision, and follow your own lab's protocols.

6
00:00:26,923 --> 00:00:29,448
Every cath case is a chain of decisions.

7
00:00:29,448 --> 00:00:33,109
Here is a seven-step approach you can reuse on every case.

8
00:00:33,559 --> 00:00:35,952
One, the patient and the indication.

9
00:00:35,952 --> 00:00:37,812
Two, the pre-cath checklist.

10
00:00:37,812 --> 00:00:39,341
Three, arterial access.

11
00:00:39,791 --> 00:00:41,938
Four, the diagnostic angiogram.

12
00:00:41,938 --> 00:00:44,847
Five, reading the films and making a plan.

13
00:00:45,297 --> 00:00:46,758
Six, the PCI itself.

14
00:00:46,758 --> 00:00:52,017
And seven, finishing well: the result, complications, and documentation.

15
00:00:52,467 --> 00:00:56,349
At the end, you'll get a practical plan for building these skills yourself.

16
00:00:58,049 --> 00:01:00,995
Step one: the patient and the indication.

17
00:01:00,995 --> 00:01:08,609
A woman in her sixties with type 2 diabetes had an acute anterior wall ST elevation myocardial infarction.

18
00:01:09,059 --> 00:01:14,361
Anterior wall means ST elevation across the precordial leads, V1 to V4.

19
00:01:14,361 --> 00:01:20,558
That points to the left anterior descending artery, the LAD, as the likely culprit.

20
00:01:21,008 --> 00:01:29,968
Her echo showed mildly reduced left ventricular function, an ejection fraction of forty-seven percent, with mild mitral regurgitation.

21
00:01:30,418 --> 00:01:33,941
She was treated with a pharmaco-invasive strategy.

22
00:01:33,941 --> 00:01:41,127
When primary PCI can't be delivered within about 120 minutes of diagnosis, we give fibrinolysis first.

23
00:01:41,577 --> 00:01:54,292
If lysis fails, meaning less than fifty percent ST resolution at sixty to ninety minutes, or if there's shock, arrhythmia, or ongoing ischemia, the patient goes for rescue PCI immediately.

24
00:01:54,742 --> 00:02:02,309
If lysis succeeds, we still bring the patient to the lab routinely, within 2 to 24 hours, to deal with the culprit lesion.

25
00:02:02,309 --> 00:02:04,107
That's where our case begins.

26
00:02:05,807 --> 00:02:07,773
Step two: the pre-cath checklist.

27
00:02:07,773 --> 00:02:11,823
Most complications are prevented before the needle touches the skin.

28
00:02:12,273 --> 00:02:14,045
Confirm informed consent.

29
00:02:14,045 --> 00:02:15,534
Check renal function.

30
00:02:15,534 --> 00:02:23,047
She is diabetic, so contrast kidney injury is a real risk: plan hydration, and keep contrast to a minimum.

31
00:02:23,497 --> 00:02:28,245
Check hemoglobin, platelets and coagulation, especially after a lytic.

32
00:02:28,245 --> 00:02:30,077
Ask about contrast allergy.

33
00:02:30,077 --> 00:02:35,571
Make sure antiplatelet and anticoagulant doses, and their timing, are documented.

34
00:02:36,021 --> 00:02:40,800
Feel the radial pulse, know where your bail-out kit is, and finish with a team time-out.

35
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:43,702
Step three: access.

36
00:02:43,702 --> 00:02:54,959
The note opens with a shorthand that means: under strict aseptic precautions, under local anaesthesia, the right radial artery was punctured and secured with a six French sheath.

37
00:02:55,409 --> 00:02:56,138
Why radial?

38
00:02:56,138 --> 00:03:06,481
In acute coronary syndromes, radial access means less bleeding and fewer access-site complications than femoral, and guidelines recommend it as the default.

39
00:03:06,931 --> 00:03:12,499
Puncture one to two centimetres above the radial styloid, where the artery is superficial.

40
00:03:12,499 --> 00:03:19,923
After a little local anaesthetic, advance the needle at thirty to forty-five degrees until you see a flashback of blood.

41
00:03:20,373 --> 00:03:21,629
Pass the wire gently.

42
00:03:21,629 --> 00:03:23,543
It should never meet resistance.

43
00:03:23,543 --> 00:03:25,877
Then slide the sheath in over the wire.

44
00:03:26,327 --> 00:03:31,733
Through the sheath, give a spasmolytic cocktail and heparin, per your lab's protocol.

45
00:03:31,733 --> 00:03:35,867
The diagnostic note records twenty-five hundred units of heparin.

46
00:03:35,867 --> 00:03:40,065
Heparin also protects the radial artery from occluding afterwards.

47
00:03:40,515 --> 00:03:45,283
If the catheter feels sticky or the patient has forearm pain, think spasm.

48
00:03:45,283 --> 00:03:50,051
Stop, give more vasodilator, and never pull hard against a spastic artery.

49
00:03:51,751 --> 00:03:54,210
Step four: the diagnostic angiogram.

50
00:03:54,210 --> 00:04:00,562
From the right radial, a single five French Tiger catheter can engage both coronary arteries.

51
00:04:01,012 --> 00:04:09,375
It travels up the radial and brachial arteries into the subclavian, then down into the ascending aorta, just above the coronary ostia.

52
00:04:09,825 --> 00:04:13,153
Watch the pressure trace before every injection.

53
00:04:13,153 --> 00:04:15,718
Aortic pressure here was 140 over 80.

54
00:04:15,718 --> 00:04:19,115
If the pressure dampens as you engage, pull back.

55
00:04:19,115 --> 00:04:24,523
The catheter may be wedged in the ostium, or sitting against an ostial lesion.

56
00:04:24,973 --> 00:04:27,815
Film each artery in several angulated views.

57
00:04:27,815 --> 00:04:31,949
A lesion that looks mild in one view can be critical in another.

58
00:04:32,399 --> 00:04:38,425
For the left main and circumflex, use LAO caudal, the spider view, and RAO caudal.

59
00:04:38,425 --> 00:04:46,949
For the LAD, use cranial views: AP cranial, RAO cranial to separate the septals from the diagonals, and LAO cranial.

60
00:04:47,399 --> 00:04:54,153
For the right coronary artery, use LAO and RAO views, adding LAO cranial to open up the crux.

61
00:04:54,153 --> 00:04:58,220
This whole study used just fifty millilitres of iohexol.

62
00:04:58,220 --> 00:05:01,052
Respect contrast in a diabetic patient.

63
00:05:02,752 --> 00:05:04,789
Step five: reading the films.

64
00:05:04,789 --> 00:05:09,707
Go artery by artery, segment by segment, in the same order every time.

65
00:05:10,157 --> 00:05:14,530
The left main is normal, and divides into the LAD and the circumflex.

66
00:05:14,980 --> 00:05:21,597
The LAD is a type 3 vessel: it wraps around the apex and supplies part of the inferior wall too.

67
00:05:21,597 --> 00:05:24,836
So a large territory of myocardium is at stake.

68
00:05:25,286 --> 00:05:29,813
There's minimal plaque at the ostium, and the proximal LAD is normal.

69
00:05:29,813 --> 00:05:39,259
In the mid LAD there's a ninety-nine percent discrete, eccentric stenosis, straddling the second septal branch, with only TIMI 1 flow beyond it.

70
00:05:39,709 --> 00:05:41,655
Know TIMI flow grades cold.

71
00:05:41,655 --> 00:05:44,393
Grade zero: no flow beyond the lesion.

72
00:05:44,393 --> 00:05:49,654
Grade one: contrast passes the lesion but doesn't fill the distal vessel.

73
00:05:49,654 --> 00:05:53,041
Grade two: the distal vessel fills, but slowly.

74
00:05:53,041 --> 00:05:55,347
Grade three: normal, brisk flow.

75
00:05:55,797 --> 00:06:03,071
The circumflex is co-dominant, with a large first obtuse marginal and a left posterolateral branch, all normal.

76
00:06:03,521 --> 00:06:05,979
The right coronary is also co-dominant.

77
00:06:05,979 --> 00:06:13,164
It has a twenty to thirty percent proximal plaque with TIMI 3 flow, and gives off the posterior descending artery.

78
00:06:13,614 --> 00:06:19,993
Angiographic diagnosis: a co-dominant system with single-vessel disease of the LAD.

79
00:06:19,993 --> 00:06:24,450
The lesion matches the ECG territory, so it's our culprit.

80
00:06:24,450 --> 00:06:26,371
The plan: PCI to the LAD.

81
00:06:26,821 --> 00:06:28,513
One documentation lesson.

82
00:06:28,513 --> 00:06:33,725
The angiogram report places this lesion in the mid LAD, at the second septal.

83
00:06:33,725 --> 00:06:36,094
The PCI note calls it proximal LAD.

84
00:06:36,094 --> 00:06:45,231
By the SYNTAX segment definitions, the proximal LAD ends at the first major septal branch, so a lesion at the second septal is mid LAD.

85
00:06:45,231 --> 00:06:51,525
Reconcile segment names before you sign, because the next operator will plan from your words.

86
00:06:53,225 --> 00:06:54,175
Now the hardware.

87
00:06:54,175 --> 00:06:57,471
You should be able to say why every device is on the table.

88
00:06:57,921 --> 00:07:00,804
The guide catheter: a six French EBU 3.5.

89
00:07:00,804 --> 00:07:09,877
Extra backup guides brace against the opposite wall of the aorta, giving strong support to push balloons and stents down the LAD.

90
00:07:09,877 --> 00:07:15,926
Size the curve to the aortic root: smaller for a small root, larger for a dilated one.

91
00:07:16,376 --> 00:07:19,445
The wire: a zero point zero one four inch BMW.

92
00:07:19,445 --> 00:07:27,917
It's a workhorse wire, with a soft tip and good steering, and it's the right first choice for a non-occlusive lesion like this.

93
00:07:28,367 --> 00:07:37,178
For pre-dilation, a two by ten millimetre semi-compliant balloon, deliberately undersized, to open the lesion and make room for the stent.

94
00:07:37,628 --> 00:07:42,017
The stent: a two point five by twenty-four millimetre drug-eluting stent.

95
00:07:42,017 --> 00:07:45,204
Choose the diameter from the distal reference vessel.

96
00:07:45,204 --> 00:07:51,879
Choose the length to cover the lesion from healthy segment to healthy segment, leaving no disease at the edges.

97
00:07:52,329 --> 00:07:57,766
For post-dilation, a two point five by ten millimetre non-compliant, or NC, balloon.

98
00:07:57,766 --> 00:08:06,375
A non-compliant balloon barely grows as pressure rises, so it can expand the stent with high force without overstretching the artery.

99
00:08:06,375 --> 00:08:09,353
Keep it shorter than the stent, and inside it.

100
00:08:09,803 --> 00:08:17,669
Where available, intravascular imaging with IVUS or OCT shows the true vessel size and confirms stent expansion.

101
00:08:17,669 --> 00:08:22,304
Guidelines increasingly favour it, especially for complex lesions.

102
00:08:22,754 --> 00:08:25,692
Before the PCI, top up anticoagulation.

103
00:08:25,692 --> 00:08:42,189
Unfractionated heparin is typically seventy to one hundred units per kilogram, aiming for an activated clotting time, or ACT, of about 250 to 350 seconds, adjusted for whatever anticoagulant she received with the lytic.

104
00:08:43,889 --> 00:08:45,160
Step six: the PCI.

105
00:08:45,160 --> 00:08:48,269
Engage the left coronary with the EBU guide.

106
00:08:48,269 --> 00:08:52,790
Check that it sits coaxially and that the pressure isn't damped.

107
00:08:52,790 --> 00:08:54,769
Then take a check angiogram.

108
00:08:55,219 --> 00:08:58,002
The check angiogram confirms the critical lesion.

109
00:08:58,002 --> 00:09:04,307
Choose a working view that shows it without overlap or foreshortening, and stay in that view for the key steps.

110
00:09:04,757 --> 00:09:05,223
Wiring.

111
00:09:05,223 --> 00:09:12,157
Put a small curve on the wire tip, torque it across the lesion, and park it well down in the distal LAD.

112
00:09:12,157 --> 00:09:13,557
Keep the tip in view.

113
00:09:13,557 --> 00:09:18,623
In a wrap-around LAD, it can drift into a small apical branch and perforate.

114
00:09:19,073 --> 00:09:19,784
Pre-dilate.

115
00:09:19,784 --> 00:09:27,477
The two millimetre balloon was inflated three times, at eight, ten and ten atmospheres, for ten, ten and eight seconds.

116
00:09:27,477 --> 00:09:34,007
Watch the waist on the balloon disappear, and watch the ECG and blood pressure during each inflation.

117
00:09:34,457 --> 00:09:35,668
Position the stent.

118
00:09:35,668 --> 00:09:41,470
Line up its markers so it covers the whole lesion, with healthy landing zones at both ends.

119
00:09:41,470 --> 00:09:45,678
A small puff of contrast confirms the position before you inflate.

120
00:09:46,128 --> 00:09:46,575
Deploy.

121
00:09:46,575 --> 00:09:52,259
The stent was expanded at fourteen, then sixteen atmospheres, for ten and twelve seconds.

122
00:09:52,259 --> 00:09:56,539
Inflate steadily, hold, and deflate fully before you move anything.

123
00:09:56,989 --> 00:09:57,557
Optimize.

124
00:09:57,557 --> 00:10:06,845
The NC balloon goes inside the stent and is inflated to fourteen atmospheres for twelve seconds, to fully expand the stent against the vessel wall.

125
00:10:07,295 --> 00:10:10,302
Final check, in at least two orthogonal views.

126
00:10:10,302 --> 00:10:14,944
Here there's TIMI 3 flow, with no flap, no dissection, and no thrombus.

127
00:10:14,944 --> 00:10:19,455
Look at the stent edges, and at the septal branch you stented across.

128
00:10:19,905 --> 00:10:26,945
Impression: successful PCI to the LAD with a two point five by twenty-four millimetre drug-eluting stent.

129
00:10:28,645 --> 00:10:29,657
What can go wrong?

130
00:10:29,657 --> 00:10:34,213
Know your first move for each complication before the case starts, not during it.

131
00:10:34,663 --> 00:10:39,158
Slow flow or no-reflow is common in ST elevation infarction.

132
00:10:39,158 --> 00:10:47,100
Rule out a dissection, then give intracoronary vasodilators such as adenosine, verapamil or nitroprusside.

133
00:10:47,550 --> 00:10:50,101
Edge dissection: assess it in two views.

134
00:10:50,101 --> 00:10:53,609
A flow-limiting dissection usually needs another stent.

135
00:10:54,059 --> 00:10:58,616
Perforation: inflate a balloon at the site straight away to seal it.

136
00:10:58,616 --> 00:11:05,184
Then think covered stent or coils, get an echo for tamponade, and do pericardiocentesis if needed.

137
00:11:05,184 --> 00:11:07,797
Reversing heparin is a senior decision.

138
00:11:08,247 --> 00:11:13,111
Side-branch loss: we stented across the second septal, so confirm it still fills.

139
00:11:13,561 --> 00:11:16,893
At the wrist: spasm, hematoma, or radial occlusion.

140
00:11:16,893 --> 00:11:25,060
Use a compression band with patent hemostasis: enough pressure to stop the bleeding, while flow through the artery continues.

141
00:11:26,760 --> 00:11:28,774
Step seven: finishing well.

142
00:11:28,774 --> 00:11:32,354
Continue dual antiplatelet therapy per protocol.

143
00:11:32,354 --> 00:11:37,277
After a lytic, clopidogrel is the usual P2Y12 partner for aspirin.

144
00:11:37,727 --> 00:11:49,594
Add a high-intensity statin, an ACE inhibitor given her diabetes and reduced ejection fraction, a beta blocker where indicated, and an SGLT2 inhibitor for her diabetes.

145
00:11:49,594 --> 00:11:53,621
Repeat the echo, and refer her to cardiac rehabilitation.

146
00:11:54,071 --> 00:11:54,958
Then document.

147
00:11:54,958 --> 00:12:02,561
This note does a lot well: every device with its size, every inflation with its pressure and time, and the final result.

148
00:12:03,011 --> 00:12:18,329
To make it complete, also record the heparin dose and ACT, total contrast volume, fluoroscopy time and radiation dose, TIMI flow and percent stenosis before and after, device lot numbers, and a clear statement on complications.

149
00:12:20,029 --> 00:12:22,257
Finally, how do you build these skills?

150
00:12:22,257 --> 00:12:24,999
Think of a ladder, and climb one rung at a time.

151
00:12:25,449 --> 00:12:27,305
Rung one: anatomy and views.

152
00:12:27,305 --> 00:12:30,421
Read each angiogram before you read its report.

153
00:12:30,421 --> 00:12:32,940
Write your own findings, then compare.

154
00:12:32,940 --> 00:12:35,327
Fifty films will change how you see.

155
00:12:35,777 --> 00:12:37,474
Rung two: hardware fluency.

156
00:12:37,474 --> 00:12:38,731
Handle every device.

157
00:12:38,731 --> 00:12:42,879
Know its size, what it's compatible with, and why you'd choose it.

158
00:12:42,879 --> 00:12:46,273
Prepare balloons and load stents until it's automatic.

159
00:12:46,723 --> 00:12:49,369
Rung three: simulator and bench practice.

160
00:12:49,369 --> 00:12:54,211
Wire torque, balloon preparation, and drills for no-reflow and perforation.

161
00:12:54,661 --> 00:12:58,128
Rung four: graded hands-on steps under supervision.

162
00:12:58,128 --> 00:13:04,111
Access first, then diagnostic catheters, then wiring and balloons, and finally stenting.

163
00:13:04,561 --> 00:13:06,529
Rung five: a personal case log.

164
00:13:06,529 --> 00:13:09,958
Before each case, write your plan and your prediction.

165
00:13:09,958 --> 00:13:14,973
Afterwards, note what happened, what surprised you, and one thing you'd change.

166
00:13:14,973 --> 00:13:19,036
Track your access success, contrast volume and fluoroscopy time.

167
00:13:19,036 --> 00:13:23,036
That log becomes your own private evaluation, and it compounds.

168
00:13:24,736 --> 00:13:34,656
To recap the seven steps: patient and indication, pre-cath checklist, access, diagnostic angiogram, reading and planning, the PCI, and finishing well.

169
00:13:35,106 --> 00:13:38,633
Use this framework on your next case, and every case after it.

170
00:13:38,633 --> 00:13:39,714
See you in the lab.

